\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Forward tori prolong to cubical cochains}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Let #{k} be a commutative unital ring, let #{d\ge 1}, and let
#{N\ge 3}. Write #{X=(\mathbb Z/N\mathbb Z)^d} and let the edges of
\ref{fdip-00NU} run from #{x} to #{x+\varepsilon_i} for
#{1\le i\le \covwedge}. In the maximal prolongation of \ref{fdip-00NW},
every word with a repeated consecutive direction is zero, and
##{e_i(x)\,e_j(x+\varepsilon_i)
=-e_j(x)\,e_i(x+\varepsilon_j)
\qquad(i\neq j).}
The classes of the sorted words
#{e_{i_1}(x)\cdots e_{i_r}(x+\varepsilon_{i_1}+\cdots)}
with #{i_1<\cdots<i_r} are a #{k}-basis of the degree-#{r} forms.
Sending each such word to the dual of the corresponding oriented
cube is an isomorphism onto the cubical cochains of #{X} with
values in #{k}. Under that isomorphism the differential on functions
is the forward difference
##{(\covwedge f)(e_i(x))=f(x+\varepsilon_i)-f(x),}
and in every degree it is the cubical coboundary. For #{d=2} that
coboundary is the formula of \ref{fdip-008Z}, now over #{k}. The
case #{d=1} is the directed cycle, whose prolongation has no
#{2}-form, as in the triangle of \ref{fdip-00NX}.}

\proof{
\p{Let #{y=x+2\varepsilon_i}. Then #{N\ge 3} forces #{y\neq x} and
forces #{x\to y} to be a non-edge, so #{\delta_x\,\covwedge\delta_y=0}.
Differentiating gives #{\covwedge\delta_x\,\covwedge\delta_y=0}. Expanding the two
differentials and keeping only composable pairs leaves the single
word #{e_i(x)\,e_i(x+\varepsilon_i)}, with coefficient #{-1}. That
word is zero. For #{i\neq j} the same argument with
#{y=x+\varepsilon_i+\varepsilon_j} leaves the two words
#{e_i(x)\,e_j(x+\varepsilon_i)} and
#{e_j(x)\,e_i(x+\varepsilon_j)}, and no others. Their sum is zero.}

\p{An adjacent transposition is one of those relations, and a
repeated consecutive direction is zero. Bubble sort therefore
reduces every word to a signed sorted word or to zero. The cubical
cochains, with the exterior product of cells, extend the same
first-order calculus: the degree-#{1} cochains are the edge
coefficients, the coboundary of a function is the forward
difference, and the coboundary is the transpose of the cellular
boundary
##{\partial\sigma_{ij}(x)
=e_i(x)+e_j(x+\varepsilon_i)
-e_i(x+\varepsilon_j)-e_j(x).}
That boundary squares to zero by cancellation of
codimension-#{2} faces, a finite count with coefficients in
#{\mathbb Z}. The maximal prolongation therefore maps to this
extension. The map is the identity on functions and on edges, so a
sorted word is sent to the exterior product of those edges, the dual
of one cube. Distinct cubes are independent, so the sorted words
are independent.}

\p{The edge generator is
#{e_i(x)=\delta_x\,\covwedge\delta_{x+\varepsilon_i}}, so
##{\covwedge(e_i(x))
=\sum_p e_p(x-\varepsilon_p)\,e_i(x)
+\sum_q e_i(x)\,e_q(x+\varepsilon_i).}
Reduce by the quadratic relations and evaluate on the square
#{\sigma_{ab}(z)} with #{a<b}. The coefficient is #{+1} when the
edge is the face #{e_a(z)} or the face #{e_b(z+\varepsilon_a)}, and
it is #{-1} when the edge is #{e_a(z+\varepsilon_b)} or #{e_b(z)}.
That is #{\langle e_i(x),\partial\sigma_{ab}(z)\rangle}.}

\p{Write #{e^i=\sum_x e_i(x)}. Summing the formula for
#{\covwedge(e_i(x))} and using the anticommutator gives #{\covwedge(e^i)=0}.
Both the prolongation differential and the cubical coboundary are
graded derivations, they agree on functions, and both kill every
#{e^i}. A general form is a sum of terms
#{f\,e^{i_1}\cdots e^{i_r}} with #{i_1<\cdots<i_r}, so the two
differentials agree in every degree. For #{d=2} the square formula
is the coordinate formula of \ref{fdip-008Z}. The cancellation that
proves #{\covwedge^2=0} there uses only addition in #{k}.}
}
